      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Starting from the given equation, $x - \\frac{1}{x} = 4$, we want to find $x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3}$. Here are the steps:\n\n1. First, we cube both sides of the given equation:\n\\[ \\left( x - \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^3 = 4^3 \\]\n\n2. Expanding the left side using the binomial theorem:\n\\[ x^3 - 3 \\cdot x^2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} + 3 \\cdot x \\cdot \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 - \\left( \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^3 = 64 \\]\n\n3. Simplifying the terms on the left side:\n\\[ x^3 - 3 \\cdot x \\cdot 1 + 3 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 64 \\]\n\\[ x^3 - 3x + \\frac{3}{x} - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 64 \\]\n\n4. Now, we want to find $x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3}$, so let's rearrange the equation:\n\\[ x^3 + \\frac{1}{x^3} = 64 + 3x - 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\]\n\n5. From the given equation $x - \\frac{1}{x} = 4$, we can derive the value of $x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2}$:\n\\[ \\left( x - \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 = x^2 - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\]\n\\[ 4^2 = x^2 - 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\]\n\\[ 16 = x^2 - 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\]\n\\[ x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 18 \\]\n\n6. Now, we can find $x^3 + \\frac{1}{x^3}$ using the identity:\n\\[ \\left( x + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 = x^2 + 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\]\n\\[ 4^2 = x^2 + 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\]\n\\[ 16 = x^2 + 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\]\n\\[ x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 14 \\]\n\\[ \\left( x + \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 = 14^2 = 196 \\]\n\n7. Finally, we can solve for $x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3}$ using:\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = \\left( x - \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\left( x^2 + 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\right) = 4 \\cdot 19 = 76 \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[ x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = \\boxed{76} \\]",
